<div class="<%= layout_framework_classes %>">
  <div class="<%= layout_framework_title_classes %>">
    <%= render 'framework/page_header', page_name: :sass_mixins %>
  </div>

  <div class="<%= layout_framework_content_classes %>">
    <%= render_framework_intro_paragraph(:sass_mixins) %>

    <div class="space-y-16">
      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Screen Targeting') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Load the mixins module and wrap declarations. Each wrapped block applies only on screens that match,
            using the same grammar as the responsive utility classes (<%= render DocsRef.new(page: :responsive) %>).
            Your build needs the framework source on the load path; see <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :sass_build) %>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: '@use \'framework/mixins\' as trmnl;

.forecast-tile {
    border: 1px solid var(--black);

    // 1-bit screens: thicker rule so the dithered edge stays legible.
    @include trmnl.for-1bit {
        border-width: 2px;
    }

    // md and larger screens in portrait.
    @include trmnl.screen(\'md\', \'portrait\') {
        padding: 20px;
    }
}', language: 'scss') %>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-4">
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            A mixin wraps the current rule in the matching screen ancestor: the 1-bit block above compiles to
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">.trmnl .screen--1bit .forecast-tile</code>.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen($modifiers...)</code>: one to three modifiers
              in any order. A size modifier is mobile-first (that
              size and up). Bit depth accepts <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'1bit'</code> or
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'1'</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen-only($modifiers...)</code>: the same grammar
              with exact size targeting instead of mobile-first.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-sm</code> / <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-md</code> / <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-lg</code> and their <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">-only</code> variants: size shorthands.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-portrait</code> / <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-landscape</code>: orientation shorthands.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-1bit</code> / <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-2bit</code> / <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-4bit</code>: bit-depth shorthands.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-dark-mode</code> and <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-dark-1bit</code> / <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-dark-2bit</code> / <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-dark-4bit</code>: dark-mode targeting. Themed screens are exempt, matching the theme contract on <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :themes) %>.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-color-palette($id)</code> / <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-color-full</code>: limited-palette and full-color targeting. The palette ids are <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'3bwr'</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'3bwy'</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'4bwry'</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'6a'</code>, and <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'7a'</code>, each documented on <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :color_palettes) %>.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-density($tier)</code>: density tier targeting (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'1x'</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'2x'</code>).
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-combo</code> / <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-combo-up</code> / <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-triple</code> / <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-triple-up</code>: target an exact combination directly. <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-combo</code> takes two modifiers, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-triple</code> takes three, and the <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">-up</code> variants are mobile-first; <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen()</code> and <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen-only()</code> call these for you.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Scale Functions') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Each function wraps a pixel value in one of the framework's scale variables.
            Non-px and zero values pass through unchanged;
            lists are scaled item by item.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">content-scaled($value)</code>: multiplies by
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--content-scale</code>. Use for plugin content
              geometry.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">ui-scaled($value)</code>: multiplies by
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--ui-scale</code>, which includes device density.
              Use for geometry that should scale like the framework's own components.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-ui-scaled($value)</code>: multiplies by
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--text-ui-scale</code>, which includes density,
              Scale, and Text Scale. Use for typography.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: '@use \'framework/mixins\' as trmnl;

.forecast-tile {
    padding: trmnl.content-scaled(10px);
    // => padding: calc(10px * var(--content-scale, 1));
}', language: 'scss') %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Public Surface') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">framework/mixins</code> forwards every module the
            framework compiles itself with, which is far more than it supports. Three families are public: their
            names and signatures hold from one release to the next, and the docs cover them page by page.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <strong>Screen targeting:</strong> <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen()</code>,
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen-only()</code>, and the
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">for-*</code> shorthands listed above.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Scale functions:</strong> <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">content-scaled()</code>,
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">ui-scaled()</code>,
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-ui-scaled()</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Theme slots:</strong> the <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">framework/mixins/theme-slots</code>
              module, documented on <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :theme_slots) %> with the authoring contract on
              <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :theme_authoring) %>.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Everything else the module forwards is internal: the selector helpers
            (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">scope-selector</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">generate-screen-utilities</code>) and the variant
            emitters (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">with-all-variants</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">generate-triple-variants</code>). The internal set
            also includes the shared utility emitters, the pattern, border-level, and text-paint helpers, and the
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">trmnl-namespace</code> wrapper. They exist to compile
            the framework's own classes and can change or disappear in any release, with no deprecation notice and no
            entry in the release notes.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The same split holds inside a public module: a member the docs do not name is internal even when it sits
            next to one they do. The device configuration is public and documented on
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :sass_devices) %>; the whole SCSS contract is summarized on
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :sass_api) %>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Where the Rest Lives') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The class-based variants for markup are on <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :responsive) %>, and
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :responsive_test) %> renders mixins and classes side by side to prove they
            produce the same result.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <%= render 'framework/page_navigation' %>
  </div>
</div>
